--- a/public/chp/blog/datomworld-vs-rama.blog
+++ b/public/chp/blog/datomworld-vs-rama.blog
@@ -75,9 +75,9 @@
 
           [:p "To solve the fundamental problem of distributed computing—how to move data and computation across the network without drowning in callbacks—both Rama and Datom.world independently converge on the same mathematical tool: " [:strong "continuations"] "."]
 
-          [:p [:strong "How Rama does it:"] " Rama's dataflow API compiles internally to a Continuation-Passing Style (CPS). Developers write code that looks sequential and imperative, using operators like " [:code ":>"] " to emit values to the next step. Under the hood, Rama's compiler treats these steps as continuations, allowing the platform to transparently serialize the computation state and resume it on a different node in the cluster. This elegantly decouples the \"what\" of the computation from the \"where,\" enabling seamless cross-partition routing without callback hell."]
+          [:p [:strong "How Rama does it:"] " Rama's dataflow language is built on continuation-passing style (CPS) and compiled straight to JVM bytecode. Developers write code that looks sequential and imperative, using operators like " [:code ":>"] " to emit values to the next step. Under the hood, Rama's compiler treats these steps as continuations, allowing the platform to serialize the continuation's closure, only the variables the downstream code still uses, and invoke it on a different thread or node in the cluster. This elegantly decouples the \"what\" of the computation from the \"where,\" enabling seamless cross-partition routing without callback hell."]
 
-          [:p [:strong "How Datom.world does it:"] " Datom.world also uses continuations, but makes them explicit and mobile across trust boundaries. Through " [:a {:href "/yin.chp"} [:code "Yin.vm"]] ", a continuation is simply a reified abstract syntax tree (AST)—a pure data structure representing the remaining computation. Instead of pulling massive datasets over the network, a Datom.world peer can send a governed Yin.vm continuation directly to the node hosting the data. The remote node executes the continuation locally and returns only the final result. While Rama uses CPS to transparently route execution inside a trusted, managed cluster, Datom.world uses explicit mobile continuations to push computation to the edge in open, trustless networks."]
+          [:p [:strong "How Datom.world does it:"] " Datom.world also uses continuations, but makes them explicit and mobile across trust boundaries. Through " [:a {:href "/yin.chp"} [:code "Yin.vm"]] ", a continuation is simply a reified abstract syntax tree (AST)—a pure data structure representing the remaining computation. Instead of pulling massive datasets over the network, a Datom.world peer can send a governed Yin.vm continuation directly to the node hosting the data. The remote node executes the continuation locally and returns only the final result. Rama's continuations are implicit and compiled: they are never first-class values you can inspect, store or send, and only variable values cross the wire between the nodes of its own cluster. Datom.world's continuations are explicit data, ASTs that a peer can inspect, govern and send to the node hosting the data, which is what lets computation move across open, trustless networks."]
 
           [:h2 "Open Protocols vs. Integrated Platforms"]
 
